1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,344
How does a hardware accelerator turn two matrices into sixteen correct answers?

2
00:00:06,344 --> 00:00:09,529
Let us follow one answer: eighty.

3
00:00:09,529 --> 00:00:14,587
We are Aman Soni, Mayank Sharma, and Ankit Kumar Pandey.

4
00:00:14,587 --> 00:00:20,581
Our project is a four-by-four systolic array matrix multiplication accelerator in Verilog.

5
00:00:20,581 --> 00:00:24,703
Matrix multiplication is a core operation in AI.

6
00:00:24,703 --> 00:00:31,165
Our idea is to reuse operands as they move through sixteen processing elements,

7
00:00:31,165 --> 00:00:34,350
with several elements computing in parallel.

8
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:41,717
I am Aman, and I designed the processing element, our basic multiply-accumulate unit.

9
00:00:41,717 --> 00:00:49,007
For our chosen output, the first row of A meets the first column of B.

10
00:00:49,007 --> 00:00:57,681
The calculation is one times sixteen, plus two times twelve, plus three times eight, plus four times four:

11
00:00:57,681 --> 00:00:58,512
eighty.

12
00:00:58,512 --> 00:01:05,341
Each element multiplies signed eight-bit inputs and adds the product to a thirty-two-bit accumulator.

13
00:01:05,341 --> 00:01:09,401
It updates only when both operands are valid.

14
00:01:09,401 --> 00:01:14,384
I also implemented reset, accumulator clear, and registered data forwarding.

15
00:01:14,384 --> 00:01:20,290
The unit testbench checks ten cases, including negative arithmetic and invalid inputs.

16
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:24,350
Mayank will show how these elements work together.

17
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:32,676
I am Mayank, and I connected the sixteen processing elements into a four-by-four array.

18
00:01:32,676 --> 00:01:38,516
A values travel to the right, while B values travel down.

19
00:01:38,516 --> 00:01:46,337
Each element keeps its own partial sum, so each eventually produces one output matrix entry.

20
00:01:46,337 --> 00:01:48,713
This is output-stationary dataflow.

21
00:01:48,713 --> 00:01:51,584
Our top-left element builds eighty,

22
00:01:51,584 --> 00:01:57,920
while other elements calculate their outputs as the data wave reaches them.

23
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:01,285
Forwarding lets neighbouring elements reuse operands.

24
00:02:01,285 --> 00:02:07,621
The important challenge is timing: matching operands must reach each element together.

25
00:02:07,621 --> 00:02:16,432
I added internal input delays of zero, one, two, and three cycles across the rows and columns.

26
00:02:16,432 --> 00:02:19,798
Valid signals follow the same delays.

27
00:02:19,798 --> 00:02:27,123
I also built a cycle-by-cycle scoreboard covering one hundred and five matrices, input gaps,

28
00:02:27,123 --> 00:02:29,004
and reset recovery.

29
00:02:29,004 --> 00:02:34,350
Ankit will show how the controller runs the complete operation.

30
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:37,758
I am Ankit Kumar Pandey.

31
00:02:37,758 --> 00:02:41,825
I implemented the controller, top-level integration, and system verification.

32
00:02:41,825 --> 00:02:45,063
An external start pulse begins the operation.

33
00:02:45,063 --> 00:02:49,961
The controller clears previous sums, feeds four sets of matrix operands,

34
00:02:49,961 --> 00:02:54,859
and allows eight drain cycles for pipeline propagation and result capture.

35
00:02:54,859 --> 00:02:57,682
Busy indicates that computation is active.

36
00:02:57,682 --> 00:03:04,241
When the output is captured, done pulses for one cycle, and the result remains available.

37
00:03:04,241 --> 00:03:07,893
The documented controller sequence spans fourteen clock cycles.

38
00:03:07,893 --> 00:03:13,622
I also created a Python reference model with forty directed and random vectors.

39
00:03:13,622 --> 00:03:19,350
Those are software reference checks; the RTL is checked by the Verilog testbenches.

40
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:23,494
Here is the complete system testbench.

41
00:03:23,494 --> 00:03:28,487
It compares all sixteen outputs against independently calculated expected values.

42
00:03:28,487 --> 00:03:32,555
Test two uses the matrices we started with.

43
00:03:32,555 --> 00:03:35,699
Its first expected output is eighty.

44
00:03:35,699 --> 00:03:42,542
The documented run shows this test passing, followed by all nine system tests passing.

45
00:03:42,542 --> 00:03:47,997
The tests include identity and zero matrices, signed extremes, consecutive operations,

46
00:03:47,997 --> 00:03:51,141
reset during computation, and output stability.

47
00:03:51,141 --> 00:03:57,521
In the report waveform, start begins the transaction, busy stays high during processing,

48
00:03:57,521 --> 00:04:00,665
and done marks the captured result.

49
00:04:00,665 --> 00:04:05,196
The first result word is eighty, completing our example.

50
00:04:05,196 --> 00:04:14,350
The recorded PE and array runs also show ten unit tests and one hundred and five matrix cases passing.

51
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:19,874
We followed eighty from four products to a checked matrix output.

52
00:04:19,874 --> 00:04:22,864
The same structure computes all sixteen results.

53
00:04:22,864 --> 00:04:27,772
Our Phase One deliverable is a Verilog RTL prototype with simulation verification.

54
00:04:27,772 --> 00:04:31,529
Next, we plan memory integration, larger matrices through tiling,

55
00:04:31,529 --> 00:04:34,903
and FPGA deployment with resource and power measurements.

56
00:04:34,903 --> 00:04:38,277
Our code, tests, and report are on GitHub.

57
00:04:38,277 --> 00:04:39,350
Thank you.
